trajectory.patch:
The `buffer overflow detected` error during the `enum_mgmt` test suite execution, specifically in `test_details`, indicates a memory corruption issue. This is likely due to how the `zcbor` library manages its state history when `zcbor_state_t` arrays are declared on the stack and passed to `zcbor_new_encode_state` or `zcbor_new_decode_state`.

The `zcbor_new_decode_state` (and `zcbor_new_encode_state`) function initializes the first element of the provided `zcbor_state_t` array (e.g., `zsd[0]`) and sets its `history` pointer to the beginning of that same array (i.e., `zsd[0].history = zsd`). Subsequently, when `zcbor_new_state` is called (e.g., from `zcbor_map_decode_bulk`), it attempts to store a new temporary state into `state->history[state->depth]`.

When `state->depth` is 0, this operation becomes `zsd[0].history[0] = tmp_state;`, which effectively means `zsd[0] = tmp_state;`. This overwrites the initial `zsd[0]` state. The critical consequence is that the `history` pointer within `zsd[0]` (which was correctly pointing to the `zsd` array) is now overwritten with the uninitialized `history` pointer from `tmp_state`.

Any subsequent call to `zcbor_new_state` will then attempt to access `zsd[0].history[1]` (or higher indices), which is now `garbage_ptr[1]`, leading to a buffer overflow and crash.

The fix involves separating the "current state" `zcbor_state_t` variable from the "history array" `zcbor_state_t` array. The `zcbor_new_encode_state` and `zcbor_new_decode_state` functions will initialize the "current state" variable, and then its `history` pointer will be explicitly set to point to the dedicated "history array". This prevents the corruption of the `history` pointer and ensures that `zcbor` operates on valid memory.

The `ZCBOR_HISTORY_ARRAY_SIZE` of 7 is sufficient for the maximum nesting depth observed in the tests; the issue is not the size itself but the corruption of the `history` pointer.

